{"input_path": "/home/arc/pvl-dev/vldb30/vldb-2022-p1159-tong.pdf", "page_index": 6, "page_count": 14, "width": 1224, "height": 1584, "model_settings": {"use_doc_preprocessor": false, "use_layout_detection": true, "use_chart_recognition": false, "use_seal_recognition": false, "use_ocr_for_image_block": false, "format_block_content": false, "merge_layout_blocks": true, "markdown_ignore_labels": ["number", "footnote", "header", "header_image", "footer", "footer_image", "aside_text"], "return_layout_polygon_points": true}, "parsing_res_list": [{"block_label": "image", "block_content": "", "block_bbox": [230, 143, 985, 354], "block_id": 0, "block_order": null, "group_id": 0, "block_polygon_points": [[230.0, 143.0], [985.0, 143.0], [985.0, 354.0], [230.0, 354.0]]}, {"block_label": "figure_title", "block_content": "Figure 4: Running example of a federated kNN query  $ (k=4) $", "block_bbox": [390, 351, 831, 369], "block_id": 1, "block_order": null, "group_id": 1, "block_polygon_points": [[390.0, 368.0], [824.0, 368.0], [830.0, 357.0], [806.0, 351.0], [427.0, 351.0], [420.0, 351.0], [409.0, 351.0], [390.0, 357.0]]}, {"block_label": "figure_title", "block_content": "Table 2: The number of basic operators in the decomposition plans of federated spatial queries. Radius-known queries only involve one secure operator (secure set union/summation) for secure result collection. Radius-unknown queries are executed in multiple rounds which require extra secure operator (secure comparison) to ensure security. Here, n is the number of silos.", "block_bbox": [104, 375, 1119, 435], "block_id": 2, "block_order": null, "group_id": 2, "block_polygon_points": [[104.0, 375.0], [1119.0, 375.0], [1119.0, 435.0], [104.0, 435.0]]}, {"block_label": "table", "block_content": "<table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Category</td><td rowspan=\"2\">Federated Spatial Query</td><td colspan=\"2\">Number of Plaintext Operator</td><td colspan=\"2\">Number of Secure Operator</td></tr><tr><td>Range Query</td><td>Range Counting</td><td>Comparison</td><td>Set Union/Summation</td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Radius-Known</td><td>Federated Range Query</td><td>n</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1/0</td></tr><tr><td>Federated Range Counting</td><td>0</td><td>n</td><td>0</td><td>0/1</td></tr><tr><td>Federated Distance Join</td><td>n|R|  $ \\dagger $</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1/0</td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Radius-Unknown</td><td>Federated kNN Query</td><td>n</td><td>$ O(n \\log \\frac{z_0}{e_0}) $  $ \\ddagger $</td><td>$ O(\\log \\frac{z_0}{e_0}) $</td><td>1/0</td></tr><tr><td>Federated kNN Join</td><td>n|R|</td><td>$ O(n|R| \\log \\frac{z_0}{e_0}) $</td><td>$ O(|R| \\log \\frac{z_0}{e_0}) $</td><td>1/0</td></tr></table>", "block_bbox": [192, 439, 1027, 618], "block_id": 3, "block_order": null, "group_id": 3, "block_polygon_points": [[192.0, 439.0], [1027.0, 439.0], [1027.0, 618.0], [192.0, 618.0]]}, {"block_label": "vision_footnote", "block_content": " $ ^{†} $ |R| is the size of the input dataset R in the federated distance join and federated kNN join.", "block_bbox": [201, 622, 881, 644], "block_id": 4, "block_order": null, "group_id": 4, "block_polygon_points": [[201.0, 622.0], [881.0, 622.0], [881.0, 644.0], [201.0, 644.0]]}, {"block_label": "vision_footnote", "block_content": " $ v_{0} $ and  $ \\epsilon_{0} $ are user-defined parameters for processing the federated kNN query and federated kNN join.", "block_bbox": [200, 646, 975, 667], "block_id": 5, "block_order": null, "group_id": 5, "block_polygon_points": [[200.0, 646.0], [975.0, 646.0], [975.0, 667.0], [200.0, 667.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "efficient execution of decomposition plans generated by the query rewriter. A driver, which consists of plaintext primitives and secure primitives, is deployed on each silo. Upon receiving a decomposition plan, plaintext operators are first executed at each silo with plaintext primitives and then secure operators are performed via the secure primitives for result assembling. As next, we elaborate on plaintext primitives (Sec. 5.1) and secure primitives (Sec. 5.2) to efficiently implement the basic operators defined in the query rewriter.", "block_bbox": [101, 682, 593, 857], "block_id": 6, "block_order": 1, "group_id": 6, "block_polygon_points": [[101.0, 682.0], [593.0, 682.0], [593.0, 857.0], [101.0, 857.0]]}, {"block_label": "paragraph_title", "block_content": "5.1 Plaintext Primitives", "block_bbox": [103, 865, 359, 887], "block_id": 7, "block_order": 2, "group_id": 7, "block_polygon_points": [[103.0, 865.0], [359.0, 865.0], [359.0, 887.0], [103.0, 887.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "The plaintext primitives implement plaintext range query and plaintext range counting. They are implemented as an interface on top of the underlying spatial databases for portability and to harness existing range query and range counting implementations.", "block_bbox": [101, 889, 594, 978], "block_id": 8, "block_order": 3, "group_id": 8, "block_polygon_points": [[101.0, 889.0], [594.0, 889.0], [594.0, 978.0], [101.0, 978.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "Primitive Implementation. The implementation of plaintext primitives is dependent on the underlying spatial databases.", "block_bbox": [101, 984, 600, 1028], "block_id": 9, "block_order": 4, "group_id": 9, "block_polygon_points": [[101.0, 984.0], [600.0, 984.0], [600.0, 1028.0], [101.0, 1028.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "For databases where range query and range counting are available, e.g., Simba [64] and PostGIS [45], we directly call the corresponding functions for plaintext range query or counting. For example, in PostGIS, a plaintext range counting on silo  $ F_{i} $ with the center p and radius r of a circular range can be implemented by calling the SQL below.", "block_bbox": [103, 1031, 593, 1162], "block_id": 10, "block_order": 5, "group_id": 10, "block_polygon_points": [[103.0, 1031.0], [593.0, 1031.0], [593.0, 1162.0], [103.0, 1162.0]]}, {"block_label": "inline_formula", "block_content": " $$ F_{i} $$ ", "block_bbox": [365, 1165, 382, 1182], "block_id": 11, "block_order": 6, "group_id": 11, "block_polygon_points": [[365.0, 1165.0], [381.0, 1165.0], [381.0, 1181.0], [365.0, 1181.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "For databases without such queries, drivers provide a default implementation of range query and range counting. For example, GeoMesa [27] only provides an interface of range query. Thus, we implement range counting by first running a range query, and then counting the cardinality of the returned set.", "block_bbox": [104, 1202, 592, 1311], "block_id": 12, "block_order": 7, "group_id": 12, "block_polygon_points": [[104.0, 1202.0], [592.0, 1202.0], [592.0, 1311.0], [104.0, 1311.0]]}, {"block_label": "display_formula", "block_content": " $$ \\mathrm{W H E R E~S T\\_{D} W i t h i n(p,~F_{i}.l o c a t i o n,~r);} $$ ", "block_bbox": [188, 1177, 490, 1202], "block_id": 13, "block_order": 8, "group_id": 13, "block_polygon_points": [[188.0, 1177.0], [490.0, 1177.0], [490.0, 1202.0], [188.0, 1202.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "Time Complexity. The time complexity of plaintext primitives depends on the native implementation in spatial databases. For example, plaintext range counting takes  $ O(\\log m) $ time with spatial indices [46], where m is the data size. Yet plaintext range query may need  $ O(\\log m + |S|) $ time, where S is the query result.\n\nDiscussions. We make two notes on the plaintext primitives.", "block_bbox": [100, 1312, 592, 1423], "block_id": 14, "block_order": 9, "group_id": 14, "block_polygon_points": [[100.0, 1312.0], [592.0, 1312.0], [592.0, 1423.0], [100.0, 1423.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "", "block_bbox": [631, 682, 1090, 703], "block_id": 15, "block_order": 10, "group_id": 14, "block_polygon_points": [[631.0, 682.0], [1090.0, 682.0], [1090.0, 703.0], [631.0, 703.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "- To support the differential privacy based acceleration for federated kNN query (see Sec. 4.5), Hu-Fu drivers provide an optional plaintext kNN query interface. The plaintext kNN is implemented by a function call on spatial databases with native kNN query (e.g., PostGIS [45] and Simba [64]).", "block_bbox": [633, 712, 1122, 822], "block_id": 16, "block_order": 11, "group_id": 16, "block_polygon_points": [[633.0, 712.0], [1122.0, 712.0], [1122.0, 822.0], [633.0, 822.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "• Since the time complexity of plaintext primitives varies, the efficiency of federated spatial queries can be limited by the slowest plaintext primitive if silos are using heterogeneous spatial databases (see Sec. 7.4). Thus, more efficient plaintext range query/counting is out of our scope.", "block_bbox": [633, 823, 1121, 932], "block_id": 17, "block_order": 12, "group_id": 17, "block_polygon_points": [[633.0, 823.0], [1121.0, 823.0], [1121.0, 932.0], [633.0, 932.0]]}, {"block_label": "paragraph_title", "block_content": "5.2 Secure Primitives", "block_bbox": [632, 951, 862, 973], "block_id": 18, "block_order": 13, "group_id": 18, "block_polygon_points": [[632.0, 951.0], [862.0, 951.0], [862.0, 973.0], [632.0, 973.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "The Secure primitives implement secure summation, comparison, and set union, which are independent of the underlying spatial databases. Recall that secure primitives take the local results from plaintext primitives as inputs. To avoid idle waiting for slow silos and to reuse local results across silos, each silo buffers its local results of plaintext primitives executed on itself. We implement secure primitives on top of such a buffer, as explained next.", "block_bbox": [629, 976, 1120, 1129], "block_id": 19, "block_order": 14, "group_id": 19, "block_polygon_points": [[629.0, 976.0], [1120.0, 976.0], [1120.0, 1129.0], [629.0, 1129.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "Primitive Implementation. Each secure primitive is implemented with a dedicated secure protocol for higher efficiency than the corresponding operation in general-purpose SMC libraries. We present the details of each secure primitive below.", "block_bbox": [629, 1135, 1123, 1221], "block_id": 20, "block_order": 15, "group_id": 20, "block_polygon_points": [[629.0, 1135.0], [1123.0, 1135.0], [1123.0, 1221.0], [629.0, 1221.0]]}, {"block_label": "text", "block_content": "Secure Summation. The implementation of secure summation is based on Ref. [19]. First, all of the $n$ silos first agree on $n$ different public parameters $U = \\{u_1, u_2, \\cdots, u_n\\}$. Then, each silo $F_i$ chooses a random $n-1$ degree polynomial $t_i(x) = (\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} a_{ik} x^k) + v_i$ and calculates $n$ values of the polynomial, $t_i(u_1), \\cdots, t_i(u_n)$. Specially, $a_{ik}$ indicates the random coefficient independently generated by silo $F_i$, and $v_i$ denotes the local counting result of silo $F_i$. These variables are held by silo $F_i$ only and kept secret from others. Afterward, each silo $F_i$ sends the value of polynomial $t_i(u_j)$ to all other $F_j(i \\neq j)$.", "block_bbox": [629, 1223, 1121, 1423], "block_id": 21, "block_order": 16, "group_id": 21, "block_polygon_points": [[629.0, 1223.0], [1121.0, 1223.0], [1121.0, 1423.0], [629.0, 1423.0]]}, {"block_label": "number", "block_content": "1165", "block_bbox": [588, 1507, 637, 1528], "block_id": 22, "block_order": null, "group_id": 22, "block_polygon_points": [[588.0, 1507.0], [636.0, 1507.0], [636.0, 1527.0], [588.0, 1527.0]]}], "layout_det_res": {"input_path": null, "page_index": null, "boxes": [{"cls_id": 14, "label": "image", "score": 0.7707136273384094, "coordinate": [230, 143, 985, 354], "order": null, "polygon_points": [[230.0, 143.0], [985.0, 143.0], [985.0, 354.0], [230.0, 354.0]]}, {"cls_id": 7, "label": "figure_title", "score": 0.8039276599884033, "coordinate": [390, 351, 831, 369], "order": null, "polygon_points": [[390.0, 368.0], [824.0, 368.0]